g0wp7kx","chapter":"surface-chemistry","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","country":"in","difficulty":null,"exam":"jee-advanced","examGroup":"jee","languages":["en"],"marks":3,"negMarks":1,"paperId":"iit-jee-2007-paper-1-offline","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2007 Paper 1 Offline","permalink":"pstatement-1--micelles-are-formed-by-surfactant-molecules-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-gwb2gfcdatl8rgaf","question":{"en":{"comprehension":null,"direction":null,"content":"<p><b>Statement 1 :</b> Micelles are formed by surfactant molecules above the critical micellar concentration (CMC).</p>\n\n<p><b>Statement 2 :</b> The conductivity of a solution having surfactant molecules decreases sharply at the CMC.</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is True, Statement 2 is NOT a CORRECT explanation for Statement 1"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is False"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is True"}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>In colloidal surfactants, critical micelle concentration (CMC) is the concentration of surfactants above which micelles. Once micelles are formed, the polar surfactants are not free to conduct electricity. Also, a micelle is much larger than a monomer it diffuses more slowly through solution and so is a less efficient charge carrier.</p>"}},"subject":"chemistry","subjectGroup":null,"timeAllotted":200000,"topic":"colloids","type":"mcq","year":2007,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2007-paper-1-offline","active":true},{"question_id":"1lg0wu4iv","chapter":"p-block-elements","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","country":"in","difficulty":null,"exam":"jee-advanced","examGroup":"jee","languages":["en"],"marks":3,"negMarks":1,"paperId":"iit-jee-2007-paper-1-offline","pape